---
// Goal: show a newcomer in one minute what to paste, whether ThinkThen fits
// their setup, and what ten functions gain them over calling a model
// directly.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import InstallLine from '../components/InstallLine.astro';
import { CODE_FUNCTIONS, FUNCTIONS, COUNTS, SURFACES } from '../data/catalog.mjs';
import { run } from '../data/samples.mjs';
import { REPO } from '../data/repo.mjs';
import overhead from '../data/overhead.json';

const shell = SURFACES.find((s) => s.slug === 'shell');
const decide = FUNCTIONS.find((f) => f.name === 'decide');
const refund = { ...run('functions/decide', '0-refund'), see: decide.see['0-refund'] };
const batching = `${REPO}/blob/main/sdlc/planning/adr/0055-decide-filter-and-rank-batches-send-each-record-once.md`;
const findRecord = `${REPO}/blob/main/specification/find.md#what-the-live-checks-measured`;
const callMs = Math.round(overhead.head.live.median);
---
<Base title="ThinkThen" description="Ten functions that ask a model plain questions and return yes, no or not sure. Pack many records into one request, and test with no API key.">
  <h1>Code that knows what you mean.</h1>
  <p class="lede">ThinkThen gives you ten functions. Each asks a model a plain question and returns yes, no or not sure. Your code branches on the answer. ThinkThen packs many records into one request: <a href={batching}>306 song titles cost 11,468 input tokens instead of 88,933</a>. Tests replay saved answers with no API key.</p>

  <InstallLine line={shell.install[0][0]} caption="Install the command" />
  <p>It fits your setup. Start in <a href="/languages/bash/">Bash</a>, then call the same functions from <a href="/languages/python/">Python</a>, <a href="/languages/typescript/">TypeScript</a> and <a href="/languages/">every other language</a>. Ask them in SQL inside <a href="/install/duckdb/">DuckDB</a>, <a href="/install/sqlite/">SQLite</a> and <a href="/install/postgresql/">PostgreSQL</a>. Point them at TypeSafe's Jev, another provider or a model on your own machine with <a href="/models/">one backend option</a>. <a href="/start/">Start</a> gives Homebrew, Windows and a try with no API key.</p>

  <Terminal run={refund} caption={refund.see} />

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Decisions your code can branch on.</b> <code>decide</code> exits 0 for yes, 1 for no and 3 for not sure, and a failure never looks like "no". <a href="/learn/answers/#exit-codes">How answers work</a></li>
    <li><b>Pay once.</b> Many records share one request. Each question keeps its own saved answer, however records group. <a href="/start/pay-once/">Pay once</a></li>
    <li><b>Test offline.</b> A test replays saved answers with no API key and no network. <a href="/start/replay/">Replay in tests</a></li>
    <li><b>Tune for free.</b> Change the threshold on saved answers with zero new calls. <code>runs audit</code> picks the threshold from your own labels. <a href="/start/tune/">Tune for free</a></li>
    <li><b>See the cost before you send.</b> <code>--plan</code> prints the first request and counts every request and its expected tokens. It sends nothing. <a href="/start/plan/">See before you send</a></li>
    <li><b>Structure from decisions.</b> <a href="/functions/recognize/"><code>recognize</code></a> pulls names out of text. <a href="/functions/find/"><code>find</code></a> picks the one line that answers, or says nothing fits. On twenty test documents it sent <a href={findRecord}>20 requests where ranking every line sent 239</a>. <a href="/functions/relate/"><code>relate</code></a> links the named things.</li>
    <li><b>Your data and keys stay put.</b> <code>--field /body</code> sends one field of each record and keeps the rest on your machine. Your API key never shows in a plan, a result, a recording or an error. ThinkThen never acts on what it reads. <a href="/start/your-data/">Your data stays yours</a></li>
    <li><b>One engine everywhere.</b> The same code runs in every language and database. One option switches the model or provider. In a library, ThinkThen's own work takes about 2 ms of a {callMs} ms call. <a href="/start/speed/">Speed</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The ten functions</h2>
  <p class="counts">{COUNTS.functions}, <a href="/languages/bash/">{COUNTS.cli}</a>, <a href="/languages/">{COUNTS.bindings}</a>.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Function</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    {CODE_FUNCTIONS.map((fn) => (
      <tr><td><a href={`/functions/${fn.name}/`}><code>{fn.name}</code></a></td><td>{fn.line}</td></tr>
    ))}
  </table>
  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/functions/question-file/">Save a question as a file</a></p>

  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/start/">Install and get a first answer</a> · <a href="/start/tutorial/">Tutorial</a> · <a href="/recipes/">Recipes</a> · <a href="/learn/beatles-bench/">Beatles Bench</a></p>
</Base>
